| WP7-D01 | SW2007 | Release guard on a `start` or `call` entry (WP7-S01) | `A release guard is allowed only on every, receive and after entries.` |
| WP7-D02 | SW2007 | Release guard type or effect (WP7-S02) | `Release guard must be bool, found {type}.` / `Release guard must be pure or read state.` (a call that writes or communicates; unresolved, remote and suspending calls keep the call rules' messages); a name outside the scope keeps the existing unresolved-name message (SW2002). |
| WP7-D03 | SW2020, warning | Release guard of an `every`, `receive` or `after` entry that folds to false in the structure where the entry is written, so the entry never runs its operation (review F3). Reported once, at the guard. Not reported when the guard names a const attribute of its component that an attribute redefinition in a specialization or usage of that component replaces (the same declaration, not only the same name), so that it may fold differently per occurrence; a guard that folds to true or does not fold gets no diagnostic. It needs a release guard, so no 2.2 source has it | `Release guard if ({guard}) of {entry} is always false; the entry never runs {operation}.` {entry} is the trigger and operation (`on every 10ms slot`, `on receive inp.go take`, `after slot next`); names are spelled as in 2.2. The fixed leading text `Release guard if (` tells it apart from WP7-D04, which shares SW2020. |
| WP7-D04 | SW2020, warning | An entry without a release guard, written in a specialization or usage, that replaces (WP7-S03) an inherited entry whose guard does not fold to true (review F2). The inherited entry is one of the effective schedule of the base (the specialized definition, or a usage's definition) or, when the usage is a part redefinition (`redefines component c: D { schedule { â€¦ } }`), of the usage it redefines (review V1); the fold is evaluated in the replacing component, so a const redefinition there decides it (review V2). Reported at the replacing entry, with the inherited entry as related information. A part redefinition that writes no replacing entry also replaces the redefined usage's entries, with their guards, by those of its own definition; it has no entry to report and is not reported. A replacement with any guard, `if (true)` included, states its own condition and is not reported; an inherited guard in a document with syntax errors is not reported elsewhere. It needs a release guard, so no 2.2 source has it | `Replacing entry {entry} drops the inherited release guard if ({guard}) of {component}, so it runs {operation} whatever the guard says; repeat the guard, or write if (true) to remove it deliberately.` {component} is the definition or usage that wrote the inherited entry. The fixed leading text `Replacing entry ` tells it apart from WP7-D03, which shares SW2020. |
